<p><strong>Summoned via PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hi there undeadmuffin. Let’s take a look at your concept.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So on the surface this seems like an overly-simple concept: you have a basic compulsion effect (the pen forces the writer to think/write whatever it’s thinking). You also have a simple page outline where you’ve listed some examples of what the pen will be writing (I’m assuming these will come across on the page as journal entries detailing the pen’s prior life).</p>
<p>I’m left wondering if that’s all to the concept… Because if so, it’s not a very compelling article on its own. You yourself have mentioned that it’s “ink pen meets Forrest Gump,”, so what’s so different about the story compared to that of the film? If you’re comparing it to a film, is there going to be a beginning, middle and end? Even, an article solely comprised of the pen’s backstory isn’t very compelling.</p>
<p>Basically, I’m pushing you to give us more. The concept on its own is workable, but the events you’ve described surrounding the anomaly isn’t very exciting at the moment.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Basically the fleeting and uncertainty of life as told by an ink pen that has gathered a number of life experiences over the decades.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is an example of an idea / theme, but your article should <em>not</em> revolve around it by itself. Rather, it should come out <em>through</em> the story that you tell. Some more questions you can consider:</p>
<p>1. Who made the pen? Why did it trap itself within a pen in the first place? What goals did the entity have before it was trapped? What does it want now besides being mopey?</p>
<p>2. How does the Foundation contain it? There should be <em>some</em> difficulty here or consequence of containment failure. Does its existential thoughts influence the researchers it comes into contact with, for example? If it’s Thaumiel, how then will the scp be used by the Foundation?</p>
<p>3. What are you trying to say by the end of the article. Right now, the message is simply: life sucks. Is that really all you want the readers to feel?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>If you decide to revise the concept further / respond below, do drop me another PM and I’ll try my best to reply. Best of luck!</p>

							</div>

								
					
